    </node>
    <node id="PILGRIMAGES DOWN THE RIVER ISS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">These are voluntary journeys undertaken by the people of Barsoom based on an ancient belief, now being challenged by the protagonist.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-151c6925632d42abc6c6f08a41de7eb8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREEN MARTIAN WOMEN">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">The Green Martian Women are associated with miraculous healing powers through their salves and lotions, representing hope for recovery and renewal.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8444ad2509b1d2679504e820feabb704</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BROAD VALLEY">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">Broad Valley is the location where John Carter and Tars Tarkas are fighting against the swarming enemy forces.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dfc552f7d0b3a308de82eacc193e6164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTUITION">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Intuition refers to the innate ability to steer and navigate without explicit guidance, relying on inner sense and trust during a perilous journey.&lt;SEP&gt;Intuition refers to the narrator's reliance on instinct and blind faith, rather than skill or reason, to navigate successfully.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-662e2b86cd0e42202773eafa992ca21e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9327b914c391d3c6243870a0c7d7b802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMPOSSIBLE BELIEF">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">A long-held belief among the people of Barsoom that is being questioned and potentially dismantled by the protagonist's actions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-151c6925632d42abc6c6f08a41de7eb8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FREEDOM">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Freedom is a central theme, representing the narrator's desire to escape servitude and tyranny, even at the cost of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Freedom is the idea of escaping imprisonment, which John Carter encourages Xodar to strive for.&lt;SEP&gt;Freedom refers to the state of being free, offered as a choice to Parthak in exchange for information.&lt;SEP&gt;Freedom represents the potential escape and liberation from a dire situation, contingent on timely action.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ad953db00d8c2abdbfeaba1f774adf47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8444ad2509b1d2679504e820feabb704&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c756f350a29d8f5307a56d2cfe02e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a0ba9e0d4459c3f9f24f06c81a870d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EMERALD BENCH">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Emerald Bench is a surface with a Barsoomian design inlaid with diamonds, part of the battle scene setting.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78737f9e0a06b70a0bb9ae9555fd2498</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLIND FAITH">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Blind Faith is the narrator's trust in an outcome without the guidance of reason or evidence, playing a critical role in their navigation.&lt;SEP&gt;Blind Faith is the reliance on belief and trust in the absence of visible guidance or reason, highlighting the psychological aspect of navigation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-662e2b86cd0e42202773eafa992ca21e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9327b914c391d3c6243870a0c7d7b802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KAB KADJA">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Kab Kadja is likely a person of authority or significance before whom the Thark is to be taken.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8547e483bbd651bfa8dda4ee339da7ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENERAL ALARM">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The General Alarm is an impending event that would alert others and potentially prevent escape, adding urgency to the situation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8444ad2509b1d2679504e820feabb704</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GOD">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">God is referenced as a divine entity, associated with pure air and possibly symbolic of hope or freedom.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3568b50dc95db579dd850d006b0923f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GREEN MARTIANS">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Green Martians are a distinct group on Mars, previously encountered by the narrator, who hold awe and respect for earthly physical abilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Green Martians are a group of beings characterized by their green appearance and the sound of their clanking accoutrements as they pursue the protagonists.&lt;SEP&gt;Green Martians are a group of mounted individuals pursuing Tars Tarkas and his companions across the Martian landscape.&lt;SEP&gt;Green Martians are another race present in the underworld, contributing to the collection of hybrids.&lt;SEP&gt;Green Martians are another species on Mars, characterized by having an intermediary set of arms between their upper and lower limbs.&lt;SEP&gt;Green Martians are depicted as hideous monsters that the Great Warrior and the Narrator are in conflict with, representing a formidable threat.&lt;SEP&gt;Green Martians are described as ferocious warriors, believed to be the most formidable in the universe.&lt;SEP&gt;Green Martians are described as having an intermediary set of arms midway between their upper and lower limbs, with protruding eyes and ears that are high set but more laterally located. Their snouts and teeth resemble those of an African gorilla, and they have an enormous shock of bristly hair on their heads.&lt;SEP&gt;Green Martians are described as warlike people who use ferocious mounts, suggesting a warrior culture and telepathic abilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Green Martians are referenced as a comparison to the ferocity of the pirates, indicating they are known for their warrior skills.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f50f91d9820d10c6c913902753e753a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce70b57630199d1d4144e52c899cfd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77936b72f93fa19de1d9822b058c9022&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ec7cb3acbeed2ca8472a8e300df6f61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8dda25a1fdacdf60f611e0ff896350d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0ff17997572867abd4f76f4d654adda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207f5996f61ac31a201fa962ab092a5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a59d3f0f2009f3a1a1e552d208e3e428&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d81037675f5b075ca39bbd7280bffb81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91f54749765e904e286c340b8839282a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE WOMAN">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">The Woman is a character who initially displays terror and incredulity upon being confronted, but her expression shifts to hope and thanksgiving.&lt;SEP&gt;The Woman is described as having a 'holy vision' and 'radiant face' but is perceived by another character as repulsive and hideous.&lt;SEP&gt;The Woman is described as very beautiful and exhibits a sense of humor despite being on the road to death. She engages in conversation about the study of lower orders and her fate.&lt;SEP&gt;The woman taught the narrator to love, a concept green Martians are reared to hate, and suffered cruel tortures and death because of her love.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aa250e31c575970c17ded33f099e84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efcfca28f5bc3f79b87ef4f83cd580c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-590f228660566d0cd39c4d36bb79722f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d4b38d0691d5aee97cd01a618d4730d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARTIAN FIGHTING SHIPS">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Martian fighting ships refer to spacecraft constructed using advanced materials like carborundum aluminum, suggesting their strength and durability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c9ca80ffc66c0dd30d63d6dd2ad3f45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NIGHT TIME">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Night time is described as a period when men sleep, and it provides a slender chance for action due to reduced vigilance on battleships.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3568b50dc95db579dd850d006b0923f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TELEPATHIC COMMUNICATION">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Telepathic Communication refers to the method by which the Green Martians guide their mounts, the thoats, without the need for physical reins or bridles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f50f91d9820d10c6c913902753e753a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIVING-ROOM">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The Living-room is a sumptuously furnished space that suggests it belongs to a high-status individual, possibly a priestess like Issus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d4b38d0691d5aee97cd01a618d4730d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SORAPUS WOOD">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Sorapus wood is a type of material used for the bench in the compartment, indicating its significance in Martian architecture.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c9ca80ffc66c0dd30d63d6dd2ad3f45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUISERS AND SMALLER CRAFT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Cruisers and smaller craft are naval vessels that rely on the vigilance of battleships for security during the night.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3568b50dc95db579dd850d006b0923f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISSUS">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Issus is a complex and multifaceted entity within the narrative, depicted as both a deity and a powerful figure with significant authority and influence over the characters and events. Revered as the Goddess of Death and Life Eternal, Issus holds a central place in the belief systems of the Martians, commanding reverence, fear, and awe. She is associated with the Temple of Issus, where she presides over monthly rites that involve combat and bloodshed, reflecting her divine status and cultural significance.

Despite being perceived as a deity, Issus is ultimately revealed to be a mortal old woman, challenging the belief in her divinity and exposing her as a cruel and wicked figure who has deceived the people for ages. Her commands are considered sacred and inviolable, and she exercises control over the fate of individuals, choosing who lives and dies, and subjecting others to imprisonment and punishment.

Issus is portrayed as a grotesque and hideous figure, sitting on a throne surrounded by soldiers and slaves, commanding and punishing those who oppose her. She is involved in the selection of individuals for meals, demonstrating her power and authority. Her presence evokes intense emotions of hate, rage, and fear, and she is a significant antagonist in the narrative, targeted for rebellion and vengeance by the protagonist and their followers.

The narrative also presents Issus as a concept related to the afterlife, reincarnation, and a false hereafter, indicating a belief or ideology that influences the characters' actions and decisions. Issus is depicted as a figure of significant cultural and religious importance, with her rites and rituals forming a key part of the story's conflict and resolution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e678ea71ac9a1264539f43637bf2a809&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad5754b925c9ad697b90bdd239dbbb10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96b7627f226c0cffd3d7951ab5750969&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f38682fe044a197c2cdd15af662eef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cc67bf329b29478328d22700eef5657&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9269232f99d91f950b2e2e1efcd036d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5122be51fbf9687e6937b89f972a742f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1403de9be5a8cd3ec91ce41c26a8925d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87b6b9321f4f05f0578fcd6c878db520&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb8bd9971e54ccc028230b4d13bef281&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4160da7474498abfc4e11860fbca61ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee174a5fe26d1ed437fbeaaf534da837&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d66810adef7e94054186f17a12de861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11af4f76d96fedbeae481784201590d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03a221bcb1631084ef422a98443917c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65cbfd544a7666d71957424825d22b07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fb7fa0605b3b0af3ae10bd81ff6ec0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac21fdcaba1072d7aae816bc5a5b7e84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3356245cc78413b113811e37a4b2050b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d75a8b07d711ce1eff0a8d3d37c746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-920a46aa7ab7990a8fd14507517ad0c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-215cc9a11b792d42bd9db23d7399186c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e73ce07604d42f9c6e9b21c0f5f3b1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23577b33daf3c193d9b0f1a6043134a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9762baf17f88ae161924df645bc3091f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a43c41b4e0cc30def80a6039700a0bca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed832c9005bcd6de678edadee8785ea2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0844027fa3550189a9d270364e85674&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ff22de37061b202fbd099af0fca2dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7f8d28a3e30c0766661860b398aad13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a4237fb784eecdbed7be6c72ee4a8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fd96469a4962b32b193a14b2cae95e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef78295a9a43f2e8c84cc7df716c9614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f872648a390de3468a26f2e0d0becad8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c6ace98e19b610b29e0db6de816d265&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7a22c0c45b96f211a7e6f3fec2419c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03e5883e77d0d77b28ad24e56e3db4f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9502067fd0cb8fc03f8323bae38b4131&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae1ccf749f2b30c16f4cc985a93939e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d4b38d0691d5aee97cd01a618d4730d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a292b6d7aae5528de2d633ec624a151c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a027e972d7eb768695014c7c960f5758&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca06d2e5dc124b584060a61efedd5b65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-510dc6fe133b57e406d839f11db76440&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cf352dbadda3cde2de75b1e9945ac7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6f2e1406ce8ebbe4ef2272e165d3aaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fbf3e1c6948f435061f05af1af6eefd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5075c6c6334565039e84371f24903659&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac8c4010c210dcbb6da5ccd528fdf874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c317929db219c46232f998050022ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd54938ae10297f91951e3dc08c03318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac274835cf15fd4d19a41f986b75a81a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af15d56fdb26a152438c0fb85843479f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13123e8cc4e42bf2ebd1e6c36837aab2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8dbd0fd5cca73331a533757f7f594757&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f392d47577a0f411eda285b198ebf39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cb36162aba338e66b3b93a517a51f9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff22eddd150fa9c82e1ebf3269b9831a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbe729ce46c2462fad11275ee94493b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6936386cb94820c7f5a584ca537f27d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cb52c113425589d349e2479393d8cbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c70f842786039ef907bfc03bffe9b7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad069d3e03018513afb20c39dd315bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da4a2f8784f599bc94defce14beef7f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c865b073d24996a602f890b25765e4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbe8483a1bbc19b7757cade3ab93f3d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-099b24e2ed3a239625f97efd0db9934c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4638e15286baadf00497251d2c1faa42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a773a771531534f350f32a0bab74d10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bc8ba319c10b95a0296d782317a73a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0409b3cde140585f12f74a86142d05cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04a29095babe1a2ff500039fc4979ce0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3964e585dee08446ad325587a718d0b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88f65b8a5a48d5153729a3de1ec4ca4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf4f2243610f4f4b76f5780dfc7ef7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c77572865c7949fb289c8ad54e38a34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f6ecb83005b0a3ed19af31b7dc7a307&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60721b3b1ff8467dd26b0e1844246065&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-979f880555ab53fcbf9eeb67a9056e54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49f2816b2fa66aa235a34337ce63c50a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4c2a87c1f4e9cc424db7febe61149a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc05f5b96f50b7c9856d78db530d1d0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ad91ced17fd76f9b7fb848f010f12b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f6195017cf2ed0308d928aeccda715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b50f9fffc90e373e4a1484b4a92e8928&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fadccafc99ba487f1ebdc2a44e91a284&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d4bd851a51f2718867c4b20dce63ae6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38627de21a3af1e2630b84098968d10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13a6342d219ff9538b029d45a991c42a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9348949c540111a72093dbdd3189e57c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449c06f1b6f91dee71f8c57cc5014980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5380aaece7d51c8e33df868c8f3f77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dceb1683460906a1f380e987a784c31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b852a9d3367f81e14d5fe18d854c6494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8827d6c7791b3b800c1f7855d869b563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eae83a9d328abc751e8b03536497faf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cacbf0b16743300060afa11c4c73b75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-085e618431c5f450074853c08198b71e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbbe716988c944d1192d640b258ae3d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-473a795cf653b75021d5abd79e52d085&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3602317d23520f84b00ad6d7b914819&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1602bdc35e6e17999a40b105c63f6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c36f83701ff3cac994524405a183c1f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-46834d00b98b366f113489159b3f44a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79bfaa16b39934f8800e01535bdd4dac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62e8976c797532ee7fe92a94f959fbbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c1de3b9f0e7f1ea967106856be68ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09e03da8ccb26edbf8fde791db77d403&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd1b65dcd4e5b65f014a78dfad395fdb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b836fd8868218c6690ae5715294518c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HOSTAGE">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">The concept of Hostage involves capturing an individual to force compliance with demands, seen as a potential strategy by the protagonist.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d4b38d0691d5aee97cd01a618d4730d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBORUNDUM ALUMINUM">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Carborundum aluminum is a light, impenetrable composition used in constructing Martian fighting ships, highlighting its importance in space engineering.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c9ca80ffc66c0dd30d63d6dd2ad3f45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIFTY THOUSAND FIGHTING MEN">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">The fifty thousand fighting men represent a diverse force gathered from various communities, ready to fight for loot and transportation back to their territories.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-049768b385a00f5c07e4449a36c4554a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE SUBMARINE">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">The Submarine is a vessel located on an island, which is strategically important and has been secured by a force.&lt;SEP&gt;The Submarine is a vessel that the message was addressed to, indicating its involvement in the current situation.&lt;SEP&gt;The Submarine is a watercraft that is moored with an uncovered hatch and is silently infiltrated by the youth and the narrator.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-11af4f76d96fedbeae481784201590d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ff22de37061b202fbd099af0fca2dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45e4a46e4701cf657ddb82656fd17c60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MORAL DUTY">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Moral Duty is the obligation felt by the narrator and others to escape and reveal the truth about the Valley of Dor and the Holy Therns to the outside world.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1c2b778334955e9ced7c09bfb06c6e52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE FLIER">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">The Flier is a small boat or vessel that the characters are attempting to reach while swimming.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f0c492fec7174d93acfd367ca61db0d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A MILLION VETERAN FIGHTING-MEN">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">A million veteran fighting men have been recruited from Helium to support the military operations, sworn to loyalty and secrecy.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-049768b385a00f5c07e4449a36c4554a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YERSTED">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Yersted is a character who has recently seen Dejah Thoris and provides information about her current status.&lt;SEP&gt;Yersted is a character who interacts with John Carter and expresses doubt about the success of Carter's mission to save the Princess.&lt;SEP&gt;Yersted is a character who interacts with the narrator and is familiar with the transportation of prisoners to Issus.&lt;SEP&gt;Yersted is a character who provides crucial information that prompts immediate action to reach the Temple of Issus.&lt;SEP&gt;Yersted is a character who provides information about the timeline concerning the presence of a woman at the foot of Issus.&lt;SEP&gt;Yersted is an individual whose information prompted urgent action, indicating their role in intelligence gathering or strategy formulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Yersted is the commander of the submarine and a former captor of John Carter. He is currently a prisoner.&lt;SEP&gt;Yersted is the commander of the submarine, having authority over operations and personnel related to the submarine.&lt;SEP&gt;Yersted is the commander of the submarine, recognized by the narrator from past interactions during captivity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55a19eca534e1c90722bcaba6bf0d0c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-214f06aa11fe94cd8053753efc32c5f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da4a2f8784f599bc94defce14beef7f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ff22de37061b202fbd099af0fca2dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d75a8b07d711ce1eff0a8d3d37c746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5380aaece7d51c8e33df868c8f3f77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23577b33daf3c193d9b0f1a6043134a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87b6b9321f4f05f0578fcd6c878db520&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9762baf17f88ae161924df645bc3091f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE BALCONY">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The Balcony is a natural formation on the cliff where the Caller stands, providing him a vantage point.&lt;SEP&gt;The balcony is a geographical location that leads into the building and is connected to an unlighted hall.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2bf5a3aa7c9e85c087d8f2ae11eff57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15f37065f3dc18c29a656f9616d653a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEDITATION">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Meditation is described as an activity that Dejah Thoris, Thuvia, and Phaidor will partake in within the Temple of the Sun.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b6eb6f891f43fb7fcb46dec56bfd5cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RADIO-AEROGRAMS">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Radio-aerograms refer to wireless communication signals that are generally avoided for secret transmissions due to the ease with which they can be intercepted and decoded by other nations.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8dc4a67f1f5f95ea1ba51770bfae3761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MARTIAN PRINCESS">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Martian Princess is an individual whom the speaker addresses with affection, indicating a close personal connection.&lt;SEP&gt;The Martian Princess is a title referring to Dejah Thoris, who holds a significant place in John Carter's heart.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2d7747bf1bf113982bf92fc681219b66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3779ce2c27b4981b2df99e660baffbea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE BUILDING">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The building is a geographical location where the hall and various apartments are situated.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-15f37065f3dc18c29a656f9616d653a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOVE">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Love is a concept taught by the woman to the narrator, despite the hatred it attracts from green Martians.&lt;SEP&gt;Love is a recurring theme, as Dejah Thoris, Thuvia, and Phaidor are all described as loving John Carter.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b6eb6f891f43fb7fcb46dec56bfd5cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aa250e31c575970c17ded33f099e84c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE TRANSPORTS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The Transports are a group of vehicles or vessels involved in a coordinated movement, likely part of a military operation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8dc4a67f1f5f95ea1ba51770bfae3761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEATH">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Death is a concept explored by the speaker, who claims to have died multiple times, yet is unable to fathom its true nature.&lt;SEP&gt;Death is personified as a force staring the narrator in the face, representing the imminent threat of mortality and the fear associated with it.&lt;SEP&gt;Death is portrayed as a frightful and horrifying end, especially as envisioned by the Holy Therns.&lt;SEP&gt;Death is the inevitable outcome that the narrator anticipates due to the overwhelming odds against them.&lt;SEP&gt;Death is the looming consequence of the struggle, as The Struggler fights for survival.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8426378e0c6a6a09748fb71be6bb2857&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89113c4da6f11aefb8678fe8526edbf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8319feaf4560fcff517d8ab936b69950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0534c79feeadfbc465eaaaa38c21aeba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d7747bf1bf113982bf92fc681219b66</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE HALL">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The hall is a broad, unlighted corridor within the building, leading to various apartments and a spiral stairway.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-15f37065f3dc18c29a656f9616d653a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIVINE ENTERTAINMENT">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Divine Entertainment refers to the cruel amusement derived from the situation of Dejah Thoris, Thuvia, and Phaidor meditating without food.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b6eb6f891f43fb7fcb46dec56bfd5cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE BATTLESHIPS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The Battleships are large military vessels that take an advanced position as part of a strategic maneuver.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8dc4a67f1f5f95ea1ba51770bfae3761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EARTH MEN">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Earth Men are humans from Earth, whose form is remarkably similar to the white apes, contributing to the protagonist's fear.&lt;SEP&gt;Earth men refers to humans from Earth, characterized by their physical attributes and cultural similarities in comparison to other beings.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e0153c5f50c46a4640a510682675ea05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8dda25a1fdacdf60f611e0ff896350d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE THREE WARRIORS">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">The Three Warriors are individuals being followed by the narrator, leading towards a cell through a series of corridors.&lt;SEP&gt;The three warriors are individuals seen leaving an apartment and being pursued down the hallway.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4ce58ae175da8c88d32eed4fe4dc295b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15f37065f3dc18c29a656f9616d653a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELESTIAL HADES">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Celestial Hades refers to a location or situation that is difficult to escape, described as a place where the characters find themselves trapped.&lt;SEP&gt;Celestial Hades refers to a metaphorical or literal challenging place from which escape is difficult, described as a stronghold with formidable defenses.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a36134f2c86fa620f5fc1afeb5c999e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c22e4d87550fcfbcdf0c1e0d337db45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE ICE CAP">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The Ice Cap refers to a geographic area over which the fleets are moving, indicating a cold and potentially treacherous environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8dc4a67f1f5f95ea1ba51770bfae3761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RADIUM REVOLVERS">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Radium revolvers are destructive weapons common on Mars, signifying advanced technology and the dangers of the setting.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e0153c5f50c46a4640a510682675ea05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATE">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Fate is described as a force that intervened to save the protagonist from harm after being pushed off the battleship.&lt;SEP&gt;Fate refers to the notion of destiny or luck that the narrator feels has given them an opportunity.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89588704ee3cae64e0a062cefc137f80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15f37065f3dc18c29a656f9616d653a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE ETERNAL HEREAFTER">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">The Eternal Hereafter refers to the mysterious and potentially spiritual destination discussed by the characters, implying an afterlife or existential journey.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-efcfca28f5bc3f79b87ef4f83cd580c8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ONE-MAN AIR SCOUTS">
      <data key="d0">ROLE</data>
      <data key="d1">One-man Air Scouts are individuals tasked with reconnaissance, flying ahead of the main fleet to protect against surprise attacks.&lt;SEP&gt;One-man air scouts are individuals responsible for protecting the team from surprise and reporting on the surroundings.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-108a9127dde1b2e1f9fc62c0742c62de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8dc4a67f1f5f95ea1ba51770bfae3761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BATTLE ETHICS">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Battle ethics refers to the rules and moral guidelines governing combat on Barsoom, emphasizing the use of similar or lesser weapons.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e0153c5f50c46a4640a510682675ea05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPIRAL STAIRWAY">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The spiral stairway is a geographical feature within the building, leading to floors above and below.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-15f37065f3dc18c29a656f9616d653a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE MOB">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The Mob is a group of religious fanatics who are infuriated by the narrator's actions against their goddess, Issus.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbbe716988c944d1192d640b258ae3d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE GREATEST JEDDAK">
      <data key="d0">ROLE</data>
      <data key="d1">The greatest Jeddak is a title referring to the most powerful ruler on Barsoom, who acknowledges John Carter's influence despite being from another world.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa38b64222e1c730376a2b084ff6bd7f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE ENEMY">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">The Enemy is an adversary engaged in a close battle, skilled in swordsmanship and filled with rage.&lt;SEP&gt;The Enemy is the attacker who orchestrates a plan that nearly results in the narrator's death, showing cunning and aggression.&lt;SEP&gt;The enemy is a Mars inhabitant, cruel in appearance, who disregards the ethical norms of battle by using a revolver against a sword.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e0153c5f50c46a4640a510682675ea05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20b2378fb16755bb8ab3dfb8a663431e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63657b9d58bb2174ebfae1cd060b738a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE GREEN WARRIOR">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">The Green Warrior is a character who is initially pursued by a herd but stands to fight back alongside the Fighting Man.&lt;SEP&gt;The Green Warrior is a character who stands in contemplation alongside the girl, considering the narrative and its implications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b90195666d478ccee815cdee51c1fb10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-325b26f02d17fd6347079d1ba4fad726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE PIT">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The Pit is a location into which the narrator and the boy fall, described as a black abyss with a polished chute leading to a dimly lit apartment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Pit is a massive circular well with smooth, basaltic rock walls, located at the crest of The Mountain, and is characterized by its immense size and inky blackness.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbbe716988c944d1192d640b258ae3d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-443e52a0d87636b2c7dc0fa86976141f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LESSER MOON">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The Lesser Moon is believed to be inhabited by mysterious beings who descend upon Barsoom.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7f7d474fe7101bd33e5c6418f6481220</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TWO-MAN FLIER">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">The two-man flier is a type of spacecraft designed to carry two ordinary men, with ray tanks charged with repulsive energy for space travel.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7965e5a361b23156242ad340f4031ff6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXPEDITION OF INVESTIGATION">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Expedition of Investigation refers to a potential journey aimed at exploring and understanding the described 'hideous mockery of heaven.'</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b90195666d478ccee815cdee51c1fb10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE ARENA">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The Arena is a confined space where a chaotic battle involving Issus' guards and armed prisoners takes place.&lt;SEP&gt;The Arena is a location beneath which the characters find themselves, indicating it is a significant structure with hidden depths.&lt;SEP&gt;The Arena is a location where events take place, including the inspection and selection of individuals by a huge black for Issus.&lt;SEP&gt;The Arena is a location where events take place, including the selection of individuals and the presence of monstrous creatures, indicating a place of spectacle and danger.&lt;SEP&gt;The Arena is an event where individuals fight, often leading to death, indicating a place of combat and survival.&lt;SEP&gt;The Arena is an underground location where the events unfold, featuring barred cages and a throne for Issus.&lt;SEP&gt;The Arena is the initial setting where the confrontation with the mob takes place, above the pit that receives Issus.&lt;SEP&gt;The Arena is the location of a deadly battle where most of the fighting-men and slave women have been killed.&lt;SEP&gt;The Arena is the location where Dejah Thoris is expected to be sent for the rites of Issus, a place associated with violence and death.&lt;SEP&gt;The Arena is the location where the battle takes place, serving as the setting for the conflict between the prisoners and the guards of Issus.&lt;SEP&gt;The Arena is the location where the events unfold, with the apes attacking the maidens.&lt;SEP&gt;The arena is the location appointed by the gods for the victims' death, indicating a place of combat and spectacle.&lt;SEP&gt;The arena is the location of the battle, filled with chaos, combat, and the clash between prisoners and guards.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8e73ce07604d42f9c6e9b21c0f5f3b1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79bfaa16b39934f8800e01535bdd4dac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3356245cc78413b113811e37a4b2050b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbbe716988c944d1192d640b258ae3d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c6ace98e19b610b29e0db6de816d265&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-093ae43827be7939bcf840af78badac5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f212e50e14d429100cb7544cff0cb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f06165f12faf40faec1cab62f00bb8fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a4237fb784eecdbed7be6c72ee4a8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e678ea71ac9a1264539f43637bf2a809&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5075c6c6334565039e84371f24903659&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96b7627f226c0cffd3d7951ab5750969&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb8bd9971e54ccc028230b4d13bef281</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE MYSTERIOUS BEINGS">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">The Mysterious Beings are rumored to inhabit the Lesser Moon, known for committing atrocities and taking prisoners.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7f7d474fe7101bd33e5c6418f6481220</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE BLACKS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The Blacks are a group of individuals depicted as having control over apes and prisoners, showing authority and influence in the setting.&lt;SEP&gt;The Blacks are a group of individuals who are adversaries in the story, showing both fury and a touch of respect for the narrator's fighting abilities.&lt;SEP&gt;The Blacks are a group or faction involved in a struggle against the red men, attempting to block their progress within the temple.&lt;SEP&gt;The Blacks are in charge of work related to exploring and remodeling the subterranean galleries beneath the arena.&lt;SEP&gt;The Blacks are the group of individuals facing the protagonist, representing an obstacle to his escape.&lt;SEP&gt;The Blacks refers to a group of attackers that pose a threat to the protagonist and his allies.&lt;SEP&gt;The blacks are described as attackers who are nearly upon the narrator and the Thark, posing a threat to them.&lt;SEP&gt;The blacks refer to the group of warriors who fight against John Carter and the reds.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d56d13213c1e66414a1713c9fa5cfdfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a4237fb784eecdbed7be6c72ee4a8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecb74cf43adde900703ca6804345ebe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0491417d3b1d17dbd4f6570ef17e2cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7965e5a361b23156242ad340f4031ff6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcd8c23863f19567a47b0d338bf750a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ef623f12c679dc785f41e5221408210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-628891acbda1427e7518f177398758d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRUTH">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Truth is the central idea that the characters aim to carry to others, despite the challenges posed by existing superstitions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b90195666d478ccee815cdee51c1fb10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JUSTICE">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Justice is a central theme in the text, representing fairness and righteousness, particularly in the context of John Carter's appeal to the men of Helium.&lt;SEP&gt;Justice is the overarching principle being demanded by Kantos Kan and the audience, seeking fair treatment and impartiality in the trial proceedings.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-18baf96786d50da1cc2652ec45a9b1cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18db334173655996e9e3de660f3dfeb6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TERRIBLE GOD">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">The Terrible God is a deity to whom the mysterious beings are said to sacrifice their victims in a gruesome ritual.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7f7d474fe7101bd33e5c6418f6481220</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LONG-SWORD">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">The Long-sword is a weapon grasped by the protagonist, symbolizing defense and readiness against the ominous presence of the Warhoons.&lt;SEP&gt;The Long-sword is a weapon used effectively by the protagonist to pierce the vitals of the Great Plant Man.&lt;SEP&gt;The long-sword is a weapon drawn from a rack, indicating its use in combat or defense.&lt;SEP&gt;The long-sword is a weapon used by the narrator to meet the attack from the blacks, indicating a defensive action.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3df18cfd0c0768045a47b07a82fd2d43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7965e5a361b23156242ad340f4031ff6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ab518ccfc750df1afb7beaaf9e5279&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-331b289581d2f7d15d72abd63236e614</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPERSTITIONS">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Superstitions refer to the impossible beliefs that the characters believe are held by mortals, hindering acceptance of their narrative.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b90195666d478ccee815cdee51c1fb10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GODDESS OF LIFE ETERNAL">
      <data key="d0">ROLE</data>
      <data key="d1">Goddess of Life Eternal is one of the titles attributed to Issus, indicating a revered and powerful position.&lt;SEP&gt;Goddess of Life Eternal is the title associated with Issus, indicating her divine status and authority.&lt;SEP&gt;The Goddess of Life Eternal is a deity with various grand titles, representing significant religious or cultural beliefs.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f87b2173e6bc1dc31b41308980db1e72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62e8976c797532ee7fe92a94f959fbbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf4f2243610f4f4b76f5780dfc7ef7c</data>
    </node>
