    <node id="THARK">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">Thark is a character who shares a mutual affection with Carthoris, indicating a strong bond between them.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is a geographical location, specifically a dead sea bottom where the battle is taking place.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is a group of people, presumably a tribe or community, that holds councils and has a leadership structure including a Jeddak.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is a group or society led by Tars Tarkas, likely a warrior clan on Barsoom.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is a group or tribe led by Tars Tarkas, indicating a structured community with a strong leader.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is a group or tribe of green warriors, led by Tars Tarkas, known for their ferocity in battle.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is a group or tribe on Barsoom, possibly known for their combat prowess and ability to fight against odds.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is a horde of green men from Barsoom known for its warriors and led by Tars Tarkas.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is a location where Tars Tarkas intends to gather support and assemble warriors if the sentiments of the people are favorable.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is a location where transports for green warriors are situated, indicating its strategic importance.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is a location, specifically a dead sea bottom, where a significant battle took place.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is a member of a tribe known for their tenacity and courage, able to fight against overwhelming odds even when severely injured.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is a region or group associated with the green men of Barsoom, led by Tars Tarkas as Jeddak.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is a significant figure that the narrator is trying to find and rescue, indicating their importance and possibly leadership role.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is a term used in the context of a cell, possibly referring to a race or group within the narrative, highlighting cultural or societal elements.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is a title or role associated with Tars Tarkas, indicating his position within the Martian society.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is described as a cruel and heartless character who has learned the meaning of friendship.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is described as a great city that currently hosts fifty thousand fighting men from various communities and hordes, ready to sail for the Land of the First Born.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is described as a great warrior, notable for wielding a long-sword and responding quickly to danger.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is described as a huge entity, possibly a creature or being from Mars, accompanying the narrator into the chamber.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is likely a warrior or adversary involved in a battle with Tan Gama, with a cell mentioned as a location of interest.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark is possibly a term or concept referring to a group or entity involved in the narrative, which is to be taken before Kab Kadja.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark refers to a Martian race known for their warrior culture, represented by Tars Tarkas in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark refers to a cruel group or entity, which John Carter is meant to teach about friendship.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark refers to a great leader or figure commanding the green warriors in battle.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark refers to a group of green Martians who are reared to hate the concept of love.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark refers to a group of green men on Barsoom, known for their strange, cruel, loveless, and unhappy nature, often seeking death over life.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark refers to a group or entity associated with a cell, indicating a structured group possibly involved in the conflict or situation.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark refers to a group or race of beings in the text, associated with Tars Tarkas and characterized by agility and strategy.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark refers to a group or race, possibly warrior-like, that holds a position of honor, particularly in relation to Dejah Thoris.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark refers to a group or race, represented here by Tars Tarkas, who are characterized as huge green warriors.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark refers to a race of fierce green warriors on Mars, known for their combat skills and hostility towards outsiders, including John Carter during his initial encounter.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark refers to a race on Barsoom, characterized by cruelty and heartlessness, but also capable of friendship as taught by John Carter.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark refers to a race or group characterized by their warrior traits and physical prowess, as exemplified by Tars Tarkas.&lt;SEP&gt;Thark refers to a region or community associated with fierce warriors, known for their distinct customs and attributes.&lt;SEP&gt;The Thark is a character whose great weight affects the operation of the spacecraft, preventing it from rising as intended.&lt;SEP&gt;The Thark is a group or faction involved in a battle, suggesting a role in the larger conflict within the narrative.</data>
