    <node id="MARTIAN CUSTOM">
      <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
      <data key="d1">The Martian custom refers to the societal norm allowing female slaves to serve Martian men, highlighting cultural practices regarding gender roles.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff22eddd150fa9c82e1ebf3269b9831a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARTHORIS">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Carthoris is a beloved son of Dejah Thoris and a figure of immense emotional significance to the people of Helium.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a boy who is surprised by Thuvia's presence and interaction with the narrator, indicating he is part of the group.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character experiencing gloomy thoughts during the journey to Helium.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character involved in the narrative, who is summoned by the speaker and is not greeted or recognized by Zat Arrras.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character mentioned as leading a column through the corridor, indicating leadership and direction.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character present at the scene, observing the arrival of a battleship with great significance.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who appears to lead a column, indicating a role of leadership or guidance in the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who assists in freeing the narrator from chains and provides them with weapons for their return journey to the palace.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who collaborates with the narrator and Kantos Kan, leading a smaller force through the pits beneath the Temple of Issus.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who conducts reconnaissance on a one-man flier to gather information about the presence of enemy forces.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who demonstrates bravery and quick action, leading the charge into a scene of confrontation.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who demonstrates chivalry and self-sacrifice, attempting to help Thuvia and John Carter escape to safety.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who expresses a desire to accompany the narrator in entering the city to confirm the fate of Tars Tarkas.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who fights alongside the narrator for the red men of Barsoom, aiming for their total emancipation from superstition.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who has slain a guard, impacting the events of the journey back to the palace.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who has suffered a bruise on his forehead but is alive, much to the relief and gratitude of the narrator, who refers to him as their son.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who interacts with the narrator, being bid good-night before the narrator attempts to rest.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who is entrusted with delivering a harness and a short-sword to Parthak, indicating his involvement in the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who is loved by the people, potentially causing public demonstrations against charges laid upon him.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who is loved by the people, suggesting he is well-regarded and possibly a central figure in the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who is not mounted and prepares to meet the charge of the Warhoons alongside others.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who is part of the group being escorted by Kantos Kan, indicating a close relationship with the protagonist.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who openly expresses emotion upon returning to Helium, indicating a deep connection to the place and people.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who quickly comprehends the danger of rising waters and urges others to act swiftly to avoid being trapped.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who receives parting instructions from the narrator before they set forth towards the city.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who reciprocates the affection shown by Thark and is involved in the journey across the dead sea bottom.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who shares a mutual affection with Tars Tarkas and is involved in the escape from the city.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who took action to protect Thuvia by sending her thoat galloping toward the hills.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character who wishes to accompany the protagonist on a mission requiring stealth and strategy.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a character whose presence is a threat to Zat Arrras' ambitions of assuming the title of Jeddak.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a key character familiar with the pits beneath the temple and plays a significant role in leading a smaller force through strategic locations.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a key figure who is involved in the transportation of men and has knowledge of the hidden pathways, indicating his strategic importance.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a knowledgeable leader who understands the hidden ways of the tunnels and guides a group of seasoned fighting-men in their quest on Barsoom.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a member of the royal family and presides at the feast, indicating his importance and role within the family.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a person expected to arrive at the island of the submarine, indicating their involvement in the ongoing events.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a person referenced in comparison to the narrator's visitor, noted for their size and age.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a person whose fate is uncertain, causing concern for the narrator.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a person whose status of being alive and free is significant to the narrator, and who is expected to receive a note.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a prince, recognized as the son of Dejah Thoris and John Carter, indicating his noble lineage and importance to Helium.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a young character who guides John Carter through the temple in search of Issus.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is a youth leading the soldiers through the corridors to escape the rising floodwaters, demonstrating leadership and bravery.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is an individual who garners support from some officers on the _Xavarian_, indicating personal loyalty and leadership qualities.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is an individual who suggests strategic actions during the battle, such as landing prisoners on islands and using buoyance rays.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is an individual whom the narrator is trying to contact, indicating a significant relationship and shared goals.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is an individual whom the narrator is trying to inform about their whereabouts.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is associated with Hor Vastus and holds a position of significance, possibly a leader or noble figure.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is identified as the son of John Carter, found in the prison of Shador, suggesting a significant role in the narrative.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is involved in attempting to repair a vessel while traveling at high speed, indicating technical skills and resourcefulness.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is involved in attempting to repair the damage to the vessel's bow, working with tools alongside Xodar.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is left to entertain the narrator's loyal friends, suggesting a role of hospitality and support within the group.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is likely related to the group or event, with a specific side of the table associated with him, suggesting a role of importance or leadership.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is mentioned as a potential victim or prisoner of Zat Arrras, suggesting involvement in a rescue plan for Dejah Thoris.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is one of the individuals transferred from the deck of the _Xavarian_ to a lesser flier, suggesting his involvement in the events.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is one of the red nobles accompanying the narrator to the Temple of the Sun.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is part of the group that arrives through the smoke to join John Carter, indicating a close association with him.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is part of the group that travels directly to Thark on another cruiser, indicating a separate mission.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is the child of Dejah Thoris, involved in the ongoing battle and search for his mother.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is the recipient of a note, indicating a connection and communication with the narrator, and is expected to understand the narrator's situation.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is the son of Dejah Thoris and John Carter, indicating his significance in the storyline.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is the son of Dejah Thoris and is implied to be leading men in a battle within the temple precincts.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is the son of John Carter, beloved by the people of Helium, reflecting the legacy of his parents.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is the son of John Carter, involved in adventures alongside his father.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is the son of John Carter, named as a combination of his parents' names, showing significance in family legacy.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is the son of the Princess of Helium and John Carter, who brings back John Carter to Helium to cheer his mother and the people.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is the son of the narrator, found in the prison of Shador, and a member of the party.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is the son of the narrator, who becomes tragically involved in a violent encounter with his father due to a series of unfortunate events.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is the son of the narrator, who is described as lifeless at one point, but later appears with a bruise, indicating a significant and possibly traumatic event.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is the son of the narrator, who is entrusted with a discreet task involving a note that should remain confidential.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthoris is the son of the speaker, who resides in the speaker's palace and is intended to receive a note.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06c8b6e33f9e011b6bce4f39de022bf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d708df29c7481f7f2727287063d43d2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ac788a7f0b0f7041a03297b242b6cad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1a320ac63f7a94c460c1ed00df793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ff22de37061b202fbd099af0fca2dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7b33af041481ac0fa8f2a1a4f22e9d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5ec04b19f44e50aea83ee65f6472738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-910404a8e8ab7a8ecae676639f332071&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-593ac9d49ec07dc6c02dbd8f0ccab1a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-689eddfc4af926a033bc08bd8ee2a22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2689f06a45abd5998519fb85220b9bd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25b54582b37bd689e9944f79dbc5d866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe97f250482c686ce435b237250ec83f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57e6e909540039e4b0a0732f451c6013&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07e93369af7ee026526581151dd43e2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681e8c340ca9a7f2e14d3fcd3d71a3e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3806151a083dd701c29455b3a3f12e94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41692485a75fafbf159b80ea98324da3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e88229e411ecdc345e27bf0ded33876&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5e9d5b5bb51756648e7a02983977bdd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2417d5af6bb8ebfe1906e22eaeea4d53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebad39afb87513bd9b8bcf938fb8fd24&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfc3d412e05467116b42fd3c9af8d965&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975c90c687850d2c03b5c57fe3acbb46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165af99021c79dc0a6c523935a6d7b6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e2cb6922d0bff95becbc89209d302ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3feecd7b55e6730b49c641bb5d5360b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5dcfae890412722c9137f3055bdbbb40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e797ebb432bac2d42d4a50cdf4e2429&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38bc5039b174d7262ba50a2721ad87c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cacbf0b16743300060afa11c4c73b75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c17bb33df2ff9d9289d6824b20a85169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee174a5fe26d1ed437fbeaaf534da837&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b24d0bed8f7d9763208b3080b3ddeb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627d56e1e0e8e3e40a952f1e6c4de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1db711ffa750bb33d7442d11412d8e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a73fbe62c4a019aaf786458159a47b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca06d2e5dc124b584060a61efedd5b65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77b39d74f4ed30d628edc00c386979b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a77e7ac5150f49ecbdb4fdb4409a0642&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d66810adef7e