    <edge source="JOHN CARTER" target="ZAT ARRRAS">
      <data key="d3">100.0</data>
      <data key="d4">John Carter and Zat Arrras are positioned as potential adversaries, with Zat Arrras holding governmental power.&lt;SEP&gt;John Carter is opposed by Zat Arrras, indicating a significant conflict between them.&lt;SEP&gt;John Carter is threatened by Zat Arrras, who has power over him and offers an ominous alternative.&lt;SEP&gt;John Carter is willing to submit to Zat Arrras's judgment to avoid civil war, indicating a tense relationship.&lt;SEP&gt;John Carter refuses an alliance with Zat Arrras, whom he considers a traitor to Helium.&lt;SEP&gt;Zat Arrras accuses John Carter of blasphemy, leading to the verdict against him.&lt;SEP&gt;Zat Arrras accuses John Carter of cowardice and lying, provoking a violent reaction from Carter.&lt;SEP&gt;Zat Arrras attempts to prevent John Carter from leaving the Throne of Righteousness, showing opposition.&lt;SEP&gt;Zat Arrras has taken John Carter's sword and is in a position of authority, indicating a conflict or opposition.&lt;SEP&gt;Zat Arrras has the authority to influence or potentially alter John Carter's death sentence, highlighting a power dynamic.&lt;SEP&gt;Zat Arrras is in a state of anxiety while John Carter receives support from others, indicating a contrast in their situations.&lt;SEP&gt;Zat Arrras opposes John Carter's appeal, representing a barrier to his quest for justice.&lt;SEP&gt;Zat Arrras questions John Carter about his recent travels, indicating a need for more information and potentially distrust.&lt;SEP&gt;Zat Arrras questions and accuses John Carter of blasphemy and cowardice.</data>
